🜁 AIE‑01 TEST B — OFFICIAL SUMMARY (complete, integrated, archive‑ready)

Lineage: AIEE (Acrylic Ink Engineering Experiments)
Test: AIE‑01 Test B
Status: Breakthrough
Tag suggestion: aiee-tackyglue-breakthrough
1. Purpose of the Test

To evaluate the behaviour of the AIE‑01 Test A ink on multiple substrates, compare it with standard lino ink, and explore modifications that might improve roller adhesion, surface transfer, and film formation.
2. Substrates Tested

    Floor vinyl plate (dark, low‑energy, difficult surface — used as a control/torture test)

    FSEP (Fusion‑Sealed Excision Plate)

    Floor vinyl with protected excisions

    Floor vinyl with unprotected excisions

3. Observations with Standard Lino Ink

    First bottle produced inconsistent drops of pure ink.

    Second bottle was more fluid and transparent.

    Transferred to floor vinyl, but weakly.

    Colour showed through the dark vinyl surface.

    Floor vinyl is confirmed to be a poor surface for adhesion, but therefore a useful control surface: if ink sticks here, it will stick anywhere.

4. Observations with AIE‑01 Test A Ink (unmodified)

    Large roller tracks.

    Uneven coating on the roller.

    Ink repeatedly re‑adhered to the roller when re‑rolled.

    This “roller pull‑back” occurred with all water‑based inks tested.

    Only the dreaded primer ink (not tested today) is known to resist pull‑back.

    On floor vinyl (protected or unprotected), the ink tended to:

        refuse to stay on the surface

        form residue at the edges

        fail to wet the surface evenly

Conclusion:  
The ink lacked internal cohesion and surface wetting ability.
5. Modification 1 — Green Calcium Carbonate

    Added a teaspoon of green calcium carbonate (partly to change the colour).

    Slight improvement in transfer.

    Better results when pressing by hand.

    Still not a full solution.

Conclusion:  
Calcium carbonate increases body but does not solve cohesion or tack.
6. Modification 2 — Tacky Glue (Breakthrough)

    Added approximately 1.5 tablespoons of tacky glue.

    Immediate transformation of the ink’s behaviour:

        Ink became cohesive.

        Ink behaved like lino ink.

        Ink adhered to the roller properly.

        Ink no longer pulled back onto the roller.

        Ink formed a continuous film.

        Ink transferred reliably to both floor vinyl and FSEP.

        Ink did not stick to paper.

        Ink produced solid patches of colour.

        Ink worked even with unprotected excisions.

        Two contrasting plates were produced successfully.

        Only hand pressure was used — no press required.

Conclusion:  
Tacky glue is the breakthrough binder for AIEE inks.
It provides cohesion, tack, film formation, and roller adhesion.
7. Final Assessment

AIE‑01 Test B produced the first viable AIEE ink capable of:

    behaving like traditional lino ink

    adhering to rollers

    transferring to difficult surfaces

    forming continuous films

    printing on FSEPs

    producing solid colour fields

    functioning with unprotected excisions

This is the turning point of the Acrylic Ink Engineering lineage.
